notes="""<h3>More on ethics and the profession</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Ethics</b> is the study of moral principles, or of good and bad behaviour. A code of ethics states principles; <b>standards of conduct</b> set a minimum level of acceptable behaviour. A code can be rules-based or principles-based and assures the public of minimum standards.</li>
<li>Laws and ethics overlap but are not the same: some legal acts are unethical and some illegal acts (civil disobedience) can be ethical.</li>
<li>Challenges to ethical behaviour arise mostly from <b>situational influences</b> and overconfidence.</li>
<li>Unethical behaviour harms clients, employers and society; it raises risk and the <b>cost of capital</b> and leads to poorer allocation of capital.</li>
<li>A profession has specialized knowledge, a code of ethics, high standards of practice, monitoring of conduct and members who see their work as serving others.</li>
<li>A decision-making framework helps identify facts, consider influences and alternatives (including unintended consequences), decide and act, then reflect.</li>
</ul>""",
